1) Data Preparation & Exploration:
=>Converted date column to datetime for time-series consistency
=>Set date as index → required for time-based modeling
=>Checked missing values and applied forward fill
=>Visualized exchange rate trend → identified non-stationarity
=>Used ADF test → confirmed need for differencing

2) Model Selection & Parameter Justification:
ARIMA:
=>Used ADF test → non-stationary → applied differencing (d=1)
=>Used ACF & PACF plots:
    -PACF → AR term (p)
    -ACF → MA term (q)
    -Selected (1,1,1) as baseline
Exponential Smoothing:
=>Used additive trend
=>No seasonality → because dataset didn’t show repeating cycles
LSTM:
Used because:
=>Captures non-linear patterns
=>Handles long-term dependencies
Hyperparameters:
=>Units (32, 50, 64) → model complexity
=>Learning rate → convergence speed
=>Batch size → training stability

3) Diagnostics & Evaluation:
ARIMA Diagnostics:
=>Residual plots → check randomness
=>QQ plot → check normality
=>ACF of residuals → no autocorrelation
Evaluation Metrics:
=>MAE → average error
=>RMSE → penalizes large errors
=>MAPE → percentage error (interpretable)
=>Lower RMSE = better predictive performance

Conclusion:
=>ARIMA works well for linear patterns
=>Exponential Smoothing captures trend efficiently
=>LSTM performs better if data has complex patterns

